+# Emergency FISA request, reported headquarters opposition and the separate criminal-warrant trail
+
+**Exhibit 334 adds a contemporaneous account of an adverse headquarters assessment and internal disagreement.** It does not contain a signed headquarters decision, DOJ/OIPR disposition or court ruling. The available August 24 control, exhibit 347, supplies an explicit emergency search request and a supporting memorandum. Reading them together advances the request/response trail while leaving the actual decision basis, routing and outcome unresolved.
+
+## What the September 10 email actually supplies
+
+[Exhibit 334](https://coop.vaed.uscourts.gov/moussaoui/exhibits/defense/334.pdf) is one court-hosted page with a top-level Kiser reply, a quoted Samit update and an earlier quoted Kiser message. Physical page 1, header, displays **9/10/01 3:45PM**; the quoted Samit header displays **09/10 3:38 PM**. No time zone is visible. These are rendered message-header statements, not native headers, transport logs or independently synchronized send/receipt times. The earlier quoted message is faint; no uncertain words are reconstructed.
+
+In the quoted Samit update, first paragraph, Samit attributes to ITOS/RFU SSA Maltbie the assessment that **“we do not have enough for either a criminal search warrant or a FISA search warrant”** of Moussaoui's computer. He reports that the matter was not pursued further because of direction treating it as an INS matter, including the quoted characterization **“FBI does not have a dog in this fight”**. He immediately adds **“Of course, I strongly disagree.”** This directly documents Samit's contemporaneous report of opposition and disagreement; it is not Maltbie's original assessment, an OIPR response, a prosecutor's decision or a judicial denial. The material actually reviewed and the standards applied are not supplied here.
+
+The top-level reply expresses regret about how the matter was handled and a conditional fear about a future incident involving the same type of aircraft. That is evidence of the writer's stated concern and sympathy. It is not independent corroboration of every quoted factual assertion, a statement identifying a particular future attack, or proof of advance knowledge of September 11.
+
+## The available emergency request: August 24 exhibit 347
+
+[Exhibit 347](https://coop.vaed.uscourts.gov/moussaoui/exhibits/defense/347.pdf) contains **nine page images**: an August 24 three-page EC, followed by a six-page LHM also dated August 24. All nine were read. Physical pages **1–3** request that headquarters secure emergency authority from the FISC to search Moussaoui's effects and residence; physical page **4** opens the supporting LHM with the same request, repeated on physical page **9 / LHM page 6**. The requested scope includes property and a residence, whereas 334's reported warrant assessment specifically mentions the computer. The sources do not establish whether the later assessment covered every requested place/item or exactly this submitted version.
+
+Physical page **1**, administrative paragraph, references an August 24 telephone call from Minneapolis to headquarters. The call recording/notes, the complete native transmittal and any returned decision are separate retrieval targets. The adjacent LHM and enclosure notation support describing the visible court packet; they do not independently authenticate a native attachment/version relationship to 334's email thread.
+
+Physical page **2** says consent searches were refused and calls the request urgent because deportation was expected. Pages **2–3 and 9** also explicitly retain uncertainty about how far a possible plan had advanced or how many unidentified associates existed. These are investigators' stated reasons and uncertainties, not our findings that an identified attack plan or network existed. The request and imperative lead wording are not proof that a court application was filed, heard, granted or denied. “Emergency” in a request is not proof that a particular statutory emergency-authorization procedure was invoked.
+
+## Three routes must remain distinct
+
+| Route | Actual source and locator | Recorded act or account | Not established by these copies |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Parallel criminal investigation / prosecutor contact | [472](https://coop.vaed.uscourts.gov/moussaoui/exhibits/defense/472.pdf), August 18, pp. 23/25; [688](https://coop.vaed.uscourts.gov/moussaoui/exhibits/defense/688.pdf), August 19, p. 1 | Requests headquarters/OIPR clearance for Minneapolis to contact the Minnesota U.S. Attorney's Office and pursue criminal process. | Actual permission/refusal, prosecutor contact, application, order, search or documented no-action. |
+| Emergency FISC search request | 347, August 24, pp. 1–4/9; 334, September 10, quoted update paragraph 1 | 347 requests emergency search authority. 334 reports a headquarters assessment against both criminal and FISA warrants for the computer, and Samit's disagreement. | Authenticating that exact request-to-assessment join, decision date/basis, DOJ/OIPR submission or judicial disposition. |
+| Proposed search in France after deportation | 334, quoted update paragraphs 2–4 | Samit describes a plan to deport Moussaoui, advice about French holding/search authority, a request for FBI attendance conveyed through Paris, and RFU opposition to that attendance. | Actual deportation, French authorization or search, attendance decision, property retention/return, obtained data or whether this plan was implemented. |
+
+The two U.S. routes can concern related evidence while requiring distinct procedural decisions. The French proposal is another reported plan, not a completed substitute search. Advice about French legal authority comes through an account with a redacted source; it is not an independently inspected French legal determination. Samit also states fears about inability to convict, release, and possible return of property. Those are prospective concerns, not established later events. No redacted identity is reconstructed.
+
+## Preserve the undertaking-versus-halt tension without choosing a chronology
+
+[Exhibit 701](https://coop.vaed.uscourts.gov/moussaoui/exhibits/defense/701.pdf), September 10, physical page 2, says Minneapolis **“undertook to obtain criminal search warrants”**. The same-date 334 account says the matter was not pursued further after the reported direction and expresses continuing efforts concerning a search in France. Both formulations remain preserved. They could address different steps/times or reflect inconsistent accounts; neither explanation has been tested against the native chronology. Exhibit 701 supplies no time of day that would establish its sequence relative to the email.
+
+The previous [701 comparison](../moussaoui-sept10-request-trail-2026-10-28/README.md) correctly described the limited contents of that held source. It is not a current global finding that no account of a headquarters response has been located: 334 now supplies one. The new account still does not supply the underlying disposition record. Earlier source bytes, wording and reports have not been rewritten.
+
+## Small source variations and contrary information stay visible
+
+The 347 source uses **“Foreign Intelligence Security Court”** on physical pages **1 and 4**, but **“Foreign Intelligence Surveillance Court”** on physical page **9**. Both readings are retained as a within-packet wording variation. No cause, preferred replacement, different tribunal or legal consequence is inferred. A native draft/template and submission counterpart could test where it arose. The case-ID redaction is not filled from another exhibit.
+
+The source contains information that limits broad suspicious-training claims: 347 physical page **6 / LHM page 3**, opening paragraph, reports interest in a Heathrow-to-JFK simulator flight including navigation and communications. That is an instructor account, not an established operational target or independent capability measurement. The August 18 instructor's genuine-aviation-interest statement and all earlier plan/capability uncertainties remain preserved. Repeated instructor narratives across memoranda are dependent sources, not independent corroboration.
+
+The prior [692-versus-810 comparison](../faa-memorandum-comparison-2026-10-28/README.md) was checked and remains unchanged: 692 is an identity/associate-information request; 810 expressly aims to warn FAA. The latter's opening/2/5/6/7 sequence and absent printed pages 3–4 remain open. Neither 347's six-page LHM nor 692's seven pages have been substituted for missing 810 pages or identified as another memorandum by page count alone. The separately reported local FAA briefing remains contrary evidence to total nonwarning. Exhibit 334 neither proves FAA receipt nor identifies the complete warning chain.
+
+## What this supports, and what could change the assessment
+
+The source-supported inference is narrower than a conspiracy conclusion: a field investigator contemporaneously reported headquarters opposition to two warrant routes, disagreed, and described trying a foreign-search alternative. This is a concrete lead about decision authority, review quality and unperformed/blocked investigative steps. It does not establish an unlawful refusal, deliberate protection, coordination, concealment or foreknowledge.
+
+An incomplete or inaccurate summary of the headquarters exchange, materially different evidentiary versions, documented legal advice, a later reconsideration, or records showing that an alternative search actually proceeded could change that assessment. Those remain competing possibilities, not accepted explanations. Obtain the original exchange and decision basis rather than count Kiser's sympathetic reply or repeated summaries as independent proof.
+
+## Scope, preservation, TODOs and next available work
+
+All **10 new page images** were read, with four 300-dpi details of the faint 334 page. Four prior pages were reinspected: 472 pp. 23/25, 688 p. 1 and 701 p. 2. The earlier full August comparison was reused, not newly reread in full. All 10 new pages returned zero extracted text characters; no OCR or hidden-name reconstruction was performed. This is a selected claim-and-route comparison, not an exhaustive character diff or legal adjudication. [SOURCES.json](SOURCES.json) records original URLs, access times, bytes and SHA-256; [OBSERVATIONS.json](OBSERVATIONS.json) gives exact physical-page/paragraph and report-line locators. [REVIEW-AND-CHECKS.json](REVIEW-AND-CHECKS.json) distinguishes source preservation, visual reading and unperformed tests.
+
+[ROUTE-COMPARISON.json](ROUTE-COMPARISON.json) keeps requests, reported decisions, proposals and outcomes separate. [OPEN-TODOS.json](OPEN-TODOS.json) retains original email/header/version and recipient records, headquarters/OIPR/prosecutor/court dispositions, the August 24 call, the French/INS/Paris/RFU correspondence and actual search/property outcomes. All earlier 18 input requirements and FAA correction/administrative-disposition questions remain open. Missing inputs defer only dependent work. Court exhibits **351** and **350** are available next leads concerning an August 28 FISA exchange/draft; their actual contents have not been acquired or read here, and their titles do not prove a decision.
+
+Original PDFs, page orders, faint text and redactions remain unchanged on verified media. The public payload contains research notes and [exact text additions](TEXT-CHANGES.diff), excluding raw scans/rasters/extractions, signatures, private correspondence, contact/identification/account details and strategy. No outreach, new request, fee, mailbox access, merge, history rewrite, native case-record promotion or source replacement is part of this review.
